<spell-notebook "image usage">
	<header level="1">
		Image
		<header>An image is a graphic representation of something.</>
	</>
	<image-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-image>` renders a real `<img>`, so `src`, `alt`, `width`, `height` and `loading` work as they do on the native
			element.

			- `width` and `height` are the image's own pixel size, which reserves space before it loads; `size` sets how wide
			  it is drawn. (`width` is NOT the column `width` grammar here.)
			- An image is a block on its own line; `avatar`, `inline`, `spaced` and `vertical-align` make it sit in a line of
			  text instead.
			- `link` wraps it in a real link.
			- `<ui-images>` shares its size, shape, border and alignment with every image inside it, plain `<img>`s included.
			- Images have no events.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			A plain image takes no focus. A linked image (`link`) is a real link: **Tab** reaches it, **Enter** follows it;
			a `disabled` one is skipped.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- Give every image an `alt`. `alt=""` marks it decorative, so screen readers skip it; a missing `alt` stays
			  missing, and accessibility checkers will flag it.
			- A linked image (`link`) is a real link, named by its `alt`: describe where it goes.
			- `disabled` removes the link's `href` and sets `aria-disabled`, so it is no longer focusable.
			- `<ui-images>` is a plain box with no role; wrap it in a list or give it a label if the group means something.
			- If the element fails to render, the `<img>`, its `alt` and any link around it go: only children
			  written inside the element still show, through a bare `<slot>`. A broken group still shows its
			  images, without the group layout.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
